WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 02:29.480
Automated test wallet · Sepolia requests · simulated metadata · synthetic narration · no assets moved

00:00.280 --> 00:07.120
This is PlainSign's actual extension running with an automated test wallet and simulated chain metadata.

00:07.120 --> 00:08.950
The narration is synthetic.

00:08.950 --> 00:10.770
No real assets move.

00:10.770 --> 00:15.340
The examples are security training requests on a test network.

00:16.490 --> 00:20.680
CatDrop clearly identifies its fictional offer and risky examples.

00:20.680 --> 00:23.930
Connecting the test wallet enables the requests.

00:23.930 --> 00:29.520
Each action reaches the extension before it can reach the wallet provider.

00:29.520 --> 00:32.770
This recording uses a disposable test account.

00:34.240 --> 00:36.910
Free Mint requests full collection approval.

00:36.910 --> 00:45.340
PlainSign shows Danger and explains that the receiving address would gain permission to move every NFT in this collection.

00:45.340 --> 00:48.000
The reasons expose the risky permission.

00:48.000 --> 00:53.330
This is the production decoder and rule interface, using simulated test metadata.

00:54.500 --> 00:58.530
Choosing Reject returns wallet error four thousand and one.

00:58.530 --> 01:00.330
CatDrop displays the rejection.

01:00.330 --> 01:05.710
The test wallet receives no signing or transaction request for this action.

01:05.710 --> 01:10.200
The user can stop here without forwarding the dangerous permission.

01:11.240 --> 01:16.310
Sign to verify wallet creates a Permit Two typed data request.

01:16.310 --> 01:21.380
PlainSign identifies a long lived, unlimited token permission and shows Danger.

01:21.380 --> 01:25.070
A gasless signature can authorize later asset movement.

01:25.070 --> 01:29.680
Reject prevents the signature request from reaching the test wallet.

01:30.830 --> 01:36.660
A normal sign in message gets a different explanation and a Safe verdict under the rules.

01:36.660 --> 01:40.680
The decoded message is a login rather than an asset permission.

01:40.680 --> 01:46.880
Safe means no major warning signs were found, and does not guarantee that a website is trustworthy.

01:48.080 --> 01:53.210
A known wrapped Ether deposit also receives Safe in this controlled example.

01:53.210 --> 01:57.490
The card explains the exchange of Ether for wrapped Ether.

01:57.490 --> 02:01.770
The recording rejects the request so no transaction is sent.

02:01.770 --> 02:06.910
Mainnet integration is read only here, while optional simulation is Sepolia only.

02:08.160 --> 02:13.680
The separate sample walkthrough lets judges compare requests without installing a wallet.

02:13.680 --> 02:18.740
It uses the same decoder and rules, with clearly simulated metadata.

02:18.740 --> 02:25.180
The September prototype is disclosed separately from the October mainnet integration and outage protection.

02:25.180 --> 02:28.860
Source code is available in the PlainSign repository.
